The cleanest and most recommended way to change a `<span>` element's color when the mouse hovers over it is by using **CSS** with the `:hover` pseudo-class. 

Here are the best ways to achieve this:

### Method 1: Using CSS (Recommended)
This approach keeps your styling separate from your HTML structure and is the standard way to handle hover effects.

**HTML:**
```html
<span class="hover-text">Hover over me to change my color!</span>
```

**CSS:**
```css
/* The default state of the span */
.hover-text {
  color: black;              /* Initial color */
  transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Optional: Makes the color change smoothly */
  cursor: pointer;           /* Optional: Shows a hand pointer on hover */
}

/* The state when the mouse hovers over the span */
.hover-text:hover {
  color: #ff4757;            /* Color on hover (e.g., coral red) */
}
```

---

### Method 2: Inline CSS (For quick testing/single elements)
If you don't have access to a separate CSS stylesheet or a `<style>` block, you can write the CSS directly in your HTML using a `<style>` tag.

```html
<style>
  span.inline-hover:hover {
    color: blue;
  }
</style>

<span class="inline-hover">Hover over me!</span>
```

---

### Method 3: Using JavaScript (If you need dynamic logic)
If you need to trigger the color change dynamically or perform other actions alongside the hover, you can use JavaScript's mouse events.

**HTML:**
```html
<span id="js-hover">Hover over me!</span>
```

**JavaScript:**
```javascript
const span = document.getElementById('js-hover');

// Change color on mouse enter
span.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
  span.style.color = 'green';
});

// Revert color on mouse leave
span.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
  span.style.color = ''; // Clears the inline color to return to default
});
```

